<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Pins package restore to nuget.org alone, for this repo, regardless of what
  feeds a developer's machine-level NuGet.config happens to define.

  Two reasons, one practical and one security:

  1. Central Package Management treats "more than one source without package
     source mapping" as NU1507, and Directory.Build.props sets
     TreatWarningsAsErrors, so NU1507 is a BUILD ERROR. A contributor whose
     user-level config adds a private or local feed (common for anyone who also
     works on an Azure Artifacts-backed repo) cannot restore this solution at
     all until they pass a one-off configfile. CI is unaffected because hosted
     runners start with only nuget.org, which is exactly the state this file
     makes explicit and reproducible everywhere else.

  2. The <clear /> is the dependency-confusion guard. Without it, an additional
     feed earlier in the resolution order can serve a package with the same id
     as a public one. The poisoning happens on whichever machine runs a
     NON-locked-mode restore and regenerates packages.lock.json: the substituted
     package's contentHash is written there and committed, after which CI's
     locked-mode restore dutifully verifies against the poisoned lock file. So
     locked-mode is not the protection here — it faithfully reproduces whatever
     the first unlocked restore resolved. Note also that not every restore in CI
     is locked: the ui-tests and canary jobs run a plain `dotnet restore`, and
     for those <clear /> is the only guard.

  Adding a source: add it here rather than relying on machine config, and pair
  it with packageSourceMapping so NU1507 stays satisfied. You must ALSO add this
  file to the COPY line that precedes `dotnet restore` in each of the four
  src/*/Dockerfile files — container builds start from a clean SDK image and see
  only what is explicitly copied into the build context, so a feed added here
  but not copied there fails at image build with "Unable to find package", far
  from its cause.

  Filename casing is deliberate. NuGet.Configuration's DefaultSettingsFileName
  constant is "NuGet.Config" and Linux filesystems are case-sensitive, so a file
  named "NuGet.config" risks being skipped silently on exactly the Linux
  machines this is meant to protect. CI would not catch that: hosted runners
  start with nuget.org alone, so NU1507 never fires there whether the file is
  read or not.
-->
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
